# Amber "value" through time — data-grounded hypothesis

**Answer.** Amber was at its highest between **4000 and 3001 BCE (4th millennium BCE; CE −4000…−3001 = 4951–5950 calBP)**,
and it peaked in the interval **≈3400/3300–3000 BCE (best estimate 3300–2901 BCE, 5250–4851 calBP)**, i.e. the Middle
Neolithic plateaux of the 34th–31th centuries BCE, inside a broader high stand of **4000–3001 BCE**.

| statement | value |
|---|---|
| highest millennium (R1: interval mean of `Amber_inter`) | 4000–3001 BCE, mean **+1.488** (next usable block 3000–2001 BCE = −0.277; gap 1.77 Z) |
| highest anchor value | Z = **+1.54**, centuries start-labelled 3300, 3200, 3100, 3000 BCE |
| peak core (top-decile bins ≥ +1.49 **and** Z=1.54 platform) | **3300–2901 BCE** = 5250–4851 calBP (4 centuries) |
| broad high stand (≥ mean+1SD = +1.05, and ≥ half-max = +0.80) | **4000–2901 BCE** = 5950–4851 calBP (11 centuries, both the 1.46 and 1.54 levels) |
| smoothed maximum position | 3275 BCE (50 y) / 3250 (100 y) / 3175 (250 y) / 3243 (500 y); band ≈2900–4100 BCE |
| period correspondence (capital.csv) | MN (3400–2901 BCE) mean Z **+1.524**, 5/5 bins in the peak; EN (4300–3401 BCE) +0.617 (6/9 bins, the 4000–3501 ones at +1.46); SG −0.223, LN −0.880, OBA −0.272, YBA −0.706 |
| time uncertainty | **±100 y** (one century bin); the ordering of the two plateaux (1.46 vs 1.54, Δ = 0.08 Z) and any sub-century statement are NOT resolvable |

**Alternative millennium phasing.** With blocks anchored on the data start (4300 BCE) the maximum block is 4300–3301 BCE
(mean +1.305) — the same plateau. The two capital-only bins 4300 and 4200 BCE (Z = −1.07 each) belong to the **5000–4001 BCE**
astronomical block; including them lowers that block from +0.182 to +0.158 and cannot alter the winner (that block has only
10 % year coverage in time_series and is excluded by the pre-registered coverage rule).

**Interpretive background (not a criterion).** Against the Belau single-site openness proxy (`pollen_inter_100`) amber's
maximum occurs while openness is still low; cross-correlation is negative at every lag (r(0) = −0.43; best lag −500 y,
r = −0.33), and the apparent pollen "maximum" near 704–500 BCE is an artefact of a constant forward fill (std = 1.8e−15
beyond CE −704). Read as: amber (a social-capital indicator) is most prominent in the early/middle Neolithic, before the
late-record openness/land-use maximum. `pollen_grate_100` was excluded (definition not reproducible).

**Proxy and convention caveats.** The data contain **no price or value field**; Zamber/Amber are standardised find
abundance/density Z values (mean ≈ 0, sd ≈ 1 over the 39 century bins), so "highest value" is read as **highest relative
abundance/circulation intensity — frequency is not value**. "High positive = more amber" is an adopted convention, not a
statement made in the data dictionary. `capital.Zamber` and `time_series.Amber` are numerically identical on the 37 shared
bins, so their agreement is a consistency check, not independent confirmation. `Amber_inter` is a linear interpolation of
the 37 century anchors (max deviation from np.interp = 2e−16), so it carries no sub-century information; it was used for
interval means and smoothing only.
